\title{The Emotional Chromatic Number: Thresholds, Sandwiches and Abundance
for the Chromatic Counting Function of Social Networks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a systematic theory of the chromatic counting function of a finite social network
under an \emph{emotional floor}: the convention, imported from affect psychology, that a
meaningful palette contains at least three categories. For a finite simple graph $G$ on a
population $V$ with $|V| = n$, let $P_G(q)$ denote the number of proper $q$-colorings
(``emotionally consistent assignments''), and define the \emph{emotional chromatic number}
$\emoc(G) = \min\{q \ge 3 : P_G(q) > 0\}$.

Our results fall into four groups. (i) A structure theorem, $\emoc(G) = \max\{\chi(G),3\}$,
reducing the floored invariant to the classical chromatic number and yielding
isomorphism-invariance, edge-monotonicity and the disjoint-union law
$\emoc(G \sqcup H) = \max\{\emoc(G),\emoc(H)\}$. (ii) Order-theoretic laws for the counting
function: antitonicity in the friendship relation, monotonicity in the palette, the universal
floor $q^{\underline{n}} \le P_G(q)$, and a threshold law --- for $q \ge 3$,
$P_G(q) > 0 \iff \emoc(G) \le q$. (iii) A sandwich theorem
$\max\{\omega(G),3\} \le \emoc(G) \le \max\{\Delta(G)+1,3\}$, resting on a self-contained greedy
bound $\chi(G) \le \Delta(G)+1$, with a six-vertex witness making both inequalities strict
simultaneously. (iv) A quantitative abundance theorem: if every vertex has degree at most $d$
then $(q-d)^{n} \le P_G(q)$, sharp on the edgeless graph.

We also give a closed form for ``cliques with bystanders'',
$P(q) = q^{\underline{s}}q^{\,N-s}$ with $s=\min\{k,N\}$, and use it for a census of one hundred
networks: all have $\emoc \in [3,6]$, with distribution $63/13/12/12$ over the values $3,4,5,6$
and total emotional load $373$. A Nordhaus--Gaddum conservation law
$n \le \emoc(G)\emoc(\cbar)$ shows emotional simplicity of a network forces emotional complexity
of its complement. Three pieces of folklore attached to the informal model are corrected.
\end{abstract}

