\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
The chromatic polynomial $P_G(q)$ of a finite graph, introduced by Birkhoff in 1912, counts
proper colorings: maps from vertices to a palette of $q$ colors such that adjacent vertices
receive distinct colors. Its interpretations are numerous --- map colorings, exam timetabling,
frequency allocation, register allocation --- but all share one feature: the palette is a set of
interchangeable labels and the graph encodes a hard ``must differ'' constraint.

We take up an interpretation in which the palette is a fixed, small, psychologically motivated
list: the six basic emotions (happiness, sadness, anger, fear, disgust, surprise). Vertices are
people, edges are friendships, and a proper coloring is an assignment of one emotion to each
person such that no two friends display the same emotion --- an \emph{emotionally consistent
assignment}. Then $P_G(6)$ counts the emotionally consistent assignments available to a social
network, and the least admissible palette size measures the network's \emph{emotional demand}.

Two questions organise the paper. First, \emph{how many emotions does a network need?} Second,
\emph{given that it can be colored, how many ways are there?} The first is a threshold question
about the support of $P_G$, the second a quantitative question about its values. Our thesis is
that both are controlled, in opposite directions, by the same local statistics --- clique size
and degree.

\subsection{The emotional floor}
Affect models with fewer than three categories (valence alone) are regarded as degenerate in the
psychological literature, so we impose a floor of three on the palette. This convention has
non-trivial consequences: it collapses the whole bipartite world to a single value, it erases the
parity distinction between even and odd friendship circles at the level of the threshold (though
not of the counts), and it makes several natural statements false without the hypothesis
$q \ge 3$.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $V$ is a finite set (the \emph{population}) with $|V| = n$, and $G$ is a finite
simple graph on $V$: a symmetric, irreflexive relation $\sim_G$ (\emph{friendship}). We write
$\cbar$ for the complement (the \emph{stranger network}), $\deg_G(v)$ for the number of friends
of $v$, $\Delta(G) = \max_v \deg_G(v)$, and $\omega(G)$ for the clique number. $\chi(G)$ is the
classical chromatic number, $G \le H$ means $\sim_G \subseteq \sim_H$, and
$q^{\underline{m}} = q(q-1)\cdots(q-m+1)$ is the falling factorial, equal to $0$ when $m > q$.

\begin{definition}
For $q \in \mathbb{N}$, a map $c : V \to \{1,\dots,q\}$ is \emph{emotionally consistent} for $G$
if $c(x) \ne c(y)$ whenever $x \sim_G y$. The \emph{chromatic counting function} $P_G(q)$ is the
number of emotionally consistent assignments $c : V \to \{1,\dots,q\}$.
\end{definition}

$P_G$ agrees with the chromatic polynomial at non-negative integers; we work only with these
evaluations, so no polynomiality is assumed anywhere.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{emotional chromatic number} is
$\emoc(G) = \min\{q \in \mathbb{N} : q \ge 3 \text{ and } P_G(q) > 0\}$.
\end{definition}

The minimum is over a non-empty set, since $q = \max\{n,3\}$ always works. We record two facts
used constantly: \emph{(Floor)} $\emoc(G) \ge 3$; \emph{(Realisability)} $G$ admits a consistent
assignment with $\emoc(G)$ emotions, and if $q \ge 3$ and $G$ admits a consistent
$q$-assignment then $\emoc(G) \le q$.

\section{The structure theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Structure theorem]\label{thm:structure}
For every finite social network $G$, $\emoc(G) = \max\{\chi(G),3\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\le$) Since $V$ is finite, $G$ admits a proper coloring with $\chi(G)$ colors; enlarging the
palette to $\max\{\chi(G),3\}$ preserves properness, and that number is $\ge 3$, so
realisability gives the bound. ($\ge$) By realisability $G$ is $\emoc(G)$-colorable, so
$\chi(G) \le \emoc(G)$, and $3 \le \emoc(G)$ by the floor.
\end{proof}

Finiteness is load-bearing: for infinite graphs the chromatic number may be infinite while the
floored invariant is defined through finite palettes only, and the identity fails.

\begin{corollary}
If $\chi(G) \ge 3$ then $\emoc(G) = \chi(G)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Bipartite collapse]\label{cor:bip}
If $G$ is bipartite (equivalently $2$-colorable) then $\emoc(G) = 3$. More generally
$\emoc(G) = 3$ if and only if $G$ is $3$-colorable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
$\emoc(K_n) = \max\{n,3\}$, and $\emoc(C_n) = 3$ for every $n \ge 3$, irrespective of parity.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Functoriality]\label{prop:funct}
Let $G,H$ be finite social networks. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $G \le H \implies \emoc(G) \le \emoc(H)$;
\item $G \cong H \implies \emoc(G) = \emoc(H)$;
\item $\emoc(G \sqcup H) = \max\{\emoc(G),\emoc(H)\}$;
\item $\emoc(G) \le \max\{n,3\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) A consistent $\emoc(H)$-assignment for $H$ is one for $G$; apply realisability. (2)
Transport colorings along the isomorphism in both directions. (3) A consistent assignment for a
disjoint union is exactly a pair of consistent assignments for the parts. (4) Color everyone
distinctly.
\end{proof}

Part (3) is the formal expression of ``emotional demand is a property of the most tangled
component'': isolation costs nothing.

\section{Order-theoretic laws}

\begin{theorem}[Antitone in friendships]\label{thm:antitone}
If $G \le H$ on the same population, then $P_H(q) \le P_G(q)$ for every $q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Any assignment separating all $H$-friendships separates all $G$-friendships, so the set of
consistent assignments for $H$ is a subset of that for $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone in the palette]\label{thm:mono}
If $q \le r$ then $P_G(q) \le P_G(r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inclusion $\{1,\dots,q\} \hookrightarrow \{1,\dots,r\}$ induces $c \mapsto \iota \circ c$,
which preserves consistency (as $\iota$ is injective) and is itself injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal floor]\label{thm:floor}
For every $G$ on $n$ people and every $q$, $q^{\underline{n}} \le P_G(q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:antitone} with $H = K_n$, and note $P_{K_n}(q) = q^{\underline{n}}$ since
consistent assignments to a clique are exactly injections into the palette.
\end{proof}

The bound is vacuous when $n > q$; its content is for $n \le q$.

\begin{corollary}[Small groups are emotionally rich]
Every group of $n \le 6$ people, whatever their friendships, admits at least $6^{\underline{n}}$
consistent assignments of the six basic emotions; in particular at least $720$ when $n = 6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold law]\label{thm:threshold}
Let $q \ge 3$. Then $P_G(q) > 0$ if and only if $\emoc(G) \le q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$P_G(q) > 0$ is equivalent to $q$-colorability. If $G$ is $q$-colorable with $q \ge 3$,
realisability gives $\emoc(G) \le q$; conversely, if $\emoc(G) \le q$ then $G$ is
$\emoc(G)$-colorable, and enlarging the palette preserves properness.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The hypothesis $q \ge 3$ is necessary: a bipartite network with at least one edge has
$P_G(2) > 0$ while $\emoc(G) = 3 > 2$. This is exactly the artefact introduced by the floor.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
If $q \le r$ and $P_G(q) > 0$, then $P_G(r) > 0$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The sandwich theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Greedy bound]\label{thm:greedy}
If every person in $G$ has at most $d$ friends, then $G$ is $(d+1)$-colorable; consequently
$\chi(G) \le \Delta(G) + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
We prove the stronger partial statement by induction on a finite set $S \subseteq V$ of served
people: there is $c : V \to \{1,\dots,d+1\}$ consistent \emph{on $S$}. For $S = \emptyset$ any
constant map works. For $S \cup \{a\}$ with $a \notin S$, take $c$ consistent on $S$; the
emotions used by the friends of $a$ inside $S$ number at most $d$, so some emotion $e$ is free,
and $c[a \mapsto e]$ is consistent on $S \cup \{a\}$. Take $S = V$.
\end{proof}

The induction never inspects the structure of $G$ on $V \setminus S$; this is exactly the
flexibility a Brooks-type refinement would exploit.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:six}
$\emoc(G) \le \max\{\Delta(G)+1,3\}$. In particular, if $\Delta(G) \le 5$ then $P_G(6) > 0$ and
$3 \le \emoc(G) \le 6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Cliques force emotions]\label{thm:clique}
If $S \subseteq V$ is a set of pairwise friends, then $|S| \le \emoc(G)$; consequently
$\max\{\omega(G),3\} \le \emoc(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A consistent assignment restricted to $S$ is injective, so $|S|$ is at most the palette size;
apply to a consistent $\emoc(G)$-assignment and combine with the floor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sandwich theorem]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every finite social network,
\[
\max\{\omega(G),3\} \;\le\; \emoc(G) \;\le\; \max\{\Delta(G)+1,3\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Locally decidable window]
If $\Delta(G) \le 5$ then $\omega(G) \le 6$ and $3 \le \emoc(G) \le 6$. Moreover
$\emoc(G) \le 6$ if and only if $G$ is $6$-colorable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Hub-and-circle network]
Let $W$ be the graph on six people $\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$ in which $0,1,2,3,4$ form a five-cycle and
person $5$ (the \emph{hub}) is friends with all of $0,\dots,4$; i.e.\ the wheel $W_5$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous strictness]\label{thm:wheel}
For the hub-and-circle network,
\[
P_W(3) = 0,\quad P_W(4) = 120,\quad \Delta(W) = 5,\quad \omega(W) = 3,\quad \emoc(W) = 4,
\]
so that $\max\{\omega(W),3\} = 3 < \emoc(W) = 4 < 6 = \max\{\Delta(W)+1,3\}$: both inequalities
of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} are strict at once.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The counts are finite verifications over $3^6$ and $4^6$ assignments; the value $120$ agrees with
the classical wheel polynomial $q\bigl((q-2)^5-(q-2)\bigr)$ at $q=4$, an independent check. The
hub with two adjacent circle-dwellers is a triangle and no four people are pairwise friends, so
$\omega(W)=3$; the hub has five friends and nobody has more, so $\Delta(W)=5$. Finally
$P_W(3)=0$ excludes $\emoc(W)=3$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold}, and $P_W(4)>0$ gives
$\emoc(W)\le 4$.
\end{proof}

Structurally, the odd cycle beneath the hub is responsible: a $3$-coloring would have to
$2$-color the five-cycle after fixing the hub's emotion, which parity forbids. This is the
smallest witness that $\chi - \omega$ is unbounded.

\section{Abundance}

\begin{theorem}[Greedy abundance]\label{thm:abundance}
If every person has at most $d$ friends, then $(q-d)^{\,n} \le P_G(q)$ for every $q$, where
subtraction is truncated at zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $S \subseteq V$ let $\mathcal{A}(S)$ be the set of maps $c : V \to \{1,\dots,q\}$ consistent
on $S$. We show by induction that
$|\mathcal{A}(S)| \ge (q-d)^{|S|} q^{\,n-|S|}$. For $S = \emptyset$ this is the equality
$|\mathcal{A}(\emptyset)| = q^{n}$. For the step $S \to S \cup \{a\}$, consider the fibres of the
map $c \mapsto c[a \mapsto c_0]$ for a fixed reference color $c_0$. Inside $\mathcal{A}(S)$ each
fibre has exactly $q$ elements, since the color of $a$ is unconstrained; inside
$\mathcal{A}(S\cup\{a\})$ at most $d$ colors are forbidden for $a$, so each fibre retains at
least $q-d$ elements. Multiplying fibre-by-fibre gives the step, and $S = V$ gives the theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$(q-\Delta(G))^n \le P_G(q)$. In particular a community of $100$ people in which nobody has more
than five friends admits at least $5^{100} \approx 7.9\times 10^{69}$ consistent assignments of
ten emotions.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness]
For the friendless population $(d=0)$ the bound is attained: $P_{\overline{K_n}}(q) = q^{n}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
For $q \le d$ the bound degenerates to $0 \le P_G(q)$; the content is the regime $q > d$. In
particular no positivity is claimed for $q \le d$, and indeed positivity may fail there.
Theorems~\ref{thm:floor} and \ref{thm:abundance} are complementary: the former is strong for
small populations with large palettes, the latter for large sparse populations.
\end{remark}

\section{A closed form and a census}

\begin{definition}
For $N,k \in \mathbb{N}$ let $B_{N,k}$ be the network on $N$ people in which the first
$s = \min\{k,N\}$ are pairwise friends and the remaining $N-s$ (``bystanders'') have no friends.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
$P_{B_{N,k}}(q) = q^{\underline{s}}\cdot q^{\,N-s}$ with $s = \min\{k,N\}$, and
$\emoc(B_{N,k}) = \max\{s,3\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An assignment is consistent exactly when it is injective on the clique and arbitrary on the
bystanders, so consistent assignments biject with pairs (injection of the $s$-clique into the
palette, arbitrary map from the $N-s$ bystanders); these number $q^{\underline{s}}$ and
$q^{N-s}$. For $\emoc$, color the clique injectively and give bystanders the first emotion; the
lower bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:clique} together with the floor.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Census]
The census consists of one hundred networks: for $i = 0,\dots,49$ the friendship circle
$C_{i+3}$, and for $i = 0,\dots,49$ the clique network $B_{10,\,3+(i \bmod 4)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Census values and window]
$\emoc(C_{i+3}) = 3$ and $\emoc(B_{10,\,3+(i\bmod 4)}) = 3 + (i \bmod 4)$; in particular every
network of the census satisfies $3 \le \emoc \le 6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Load and distribution]
Let $L(i) = 3$ for $i < 50$ and $L(i) = 3+((i-50) \bmod 4)$ for $50 \le i < 100$. Then
$\sum_{i=0}^{99} L(i) = 373$ (an average of $3.73$), with
\[
\#\{i : L(i)=3\} = 63,\quad \#\{i : L(i)=4\} = 13,\quad
\#\{i : L(i)=5\} = 12,\quad \#\{i : L(i)=6\} = 12,
\]
and every value lies in $\{3,4,5,6\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Six-emotion counts]\label{thm:counts}
For the ten-person clique networks,
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
core clique size $k$ & $P_{B_{10,k}}(6)$ \\
\midrule
$3$ & $33{,}592{,}320$ \\
$4$ & $16{,}796{,}160$ \\
$5$ & $5{,}598{,}720$ \\
$6$ & $933{,}120$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
and these values are strictly decreasing in $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute into Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}: e.g.\ $6^{\underline{3}}\cdot 6^{7} = 120\cdot 279936$
and $6^{\underline{6}}\cdot 6^{4} = 720 \cdot 1296$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Census abundance, sharp]
Every clique network of the census admits at least $933{,}120$ consistent six-emotion
assignments, attained exactly by the six-clique network. (Each such network is a subnetwork of
$B_{10,6}$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:antitone} applies.)
\end{corollary}

Increasing the core clique from three to six raises $\emoc$ from $3$ to $6$ while cutting the
number of consistent six-emotion assignments by a factor of $36$: emotional demand and emotional
freedom move in opposite directions.

\begin{theorem}[The window is not universal]
For the seven-person clique, $P_{K_7}(6) = 0$ and $\emoc(K_7) = 7$.
\end{theorem}

Hence ``$\emoc \in [3,6]$'' is a property of the sample, not a theorem; the honest general
statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}.

\section{Conservation laws}

\begin{theorem}[Product injection]\label{thm:inj}
If $G$ is $a$-colorable and $\cbar$ is $b$-colorable, then $n \le ab$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For proper colorings $c$ of $G$ and $d$ of $\cbar$, the map $v \mapsto (c(v),d(v))$ is
injective: distinct people are either friends (separated by $c$) or strangers (separated by $d$).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nordhaus--Gaddum laws]
$n \le \chi(G)\chi(\cbar)$ and $n \le \emoc(G)\emoc(\cbar)$; consequently
$4n \le (\chi(G)+\chi(\cbar))^2$ and $4n \le (\emoc(G)+\emoc(\cbar))^2$, i.e.\
$\emoc(G)+\emoc(\cbar) \ge 2\sqrt{n}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Emotional duality]
If $\emoc(G) \le 6$ then $n \le 6\,\emoc(\cbar)$; in particular a hundred-person community whose
friendships require only the six basic emotions has $\emoc(\cbar) \ge 17$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sparse networks have dense stranger networks]
If $n = 100$ and $\Delta(G) \le 5$, then $\Delta(\cbar) \ge 16$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Corollary~\ref{cor:six} gives $\emoc(G) \le 6$, hence $\emoc(\cbar) \ge 17$ by emotional
duality; applying Corollary~\ref{cor:six} to $\cbar$ gives
$17 \le \max\{\Delta(\cbar)+1,3\}$, forcing $\Delta(\cbar) \ge 16$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-complementary networks]
If $G \cong \cbar$ then $n \le \emoc(G)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Neither vacuous nor tight]
On a complete network the product law is attained after the floor is stripped:
$\chi(K_n)\chi(\overline{K_n}) = n$. On the five-cycle it is strictly loose: $\overline{C_5}
\cong C_5$, so $\emoc(C_5)\emoc(\overline{C_5}) = 9 > 5$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Three corrections to the folklore}

\textbf{(F1) ``The chromatic polynomial has a root at $q=2$ for every bipartite graph.''}
Backwards. Bipartite is equivalent to $2$-colorable; a connected bipartite network with an edge
has $P_G(2) = 2$. The polynomial vanishes at $q = 2$ precisely for networks that are \emph{not}
bipartite. The correct form of the intuition is Corollary~\ref{cor:bip}: every bipartite network
sits exactly at $\emoc = 3$.

\textbf{(F2) ``$\emoc(C_n) = 2$ for even $n$ and $3$ for odd $n$.''}
True for the classical chromatic number, invisible to $\emoc$: the floor gives $\emoc(C_n) = 3$
for all $n \ge 3$. Parity survives only in the counts,
$P_{C_n}(q) = (q-1)^n + (-1)^n(q-1)$.

\textbf{(F3) ``$3 \le \emoc(G) \le 6$ for real social networks.''}
A statistical regularity of samples with small cliques and bounded degree, not a theorem: the
seven-person clique has $\emoc = 7$ and no consistent six-emotion assignment. The rigorous
replacement is Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} with Corollary~\ref{cor:six}.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Exact chromatic counting by deletion--contraction.} If $G$ has no edges return $q^{n}$;
otherwise pick an edge $uv$ and return $P_{G-uv}(q) - P_{G/uv}(q)$. The recursion terminates
since each call decreases $n+m$; the worst case is exponential but the method is exact and works
symbolically in $q$. For $n \le 8$ brute-force enumeration over $q^{n}$ assignments is a simpler
independent check.

\textbf{Greedy coloring.} Order the population arbitrarily and give each person the least emotion
unused by their already-colored friends; $O(n+m)$ time, always succeeds with $\Delta+1$ emotions.
This is the constructive core of Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy}, and its fibre-counting refinement is
Theorem~\ref{thm:abundance}.

\textbf{Threshold search for $\emoc$.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} the predicate
$q \mapsto [P_G(q)>0]$ is monotone for $q \ge 3$, so $\emoc(G)$ is found by scanning
$q = 3,4,\dots$ and stopping at the first success; at most $\max\{\Delta+1,3\}-2$ tests are
needed, and Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} supplies a certified bracket before any search begins.

\section{Discussion}

The threshold and abundance questions are governed by the same two local statistics with opposite
signs. Cliques push $\emoc$ up (Theorem~\ref{thm:clique}) and $P_G$ down
(Theorem~\ref{thm:antitone}); degree bounds push $\emoc$ down (Corollary~\ref{cor:six}) and $P_G$
up (Theorem~\ref{thm:abundance}). The census makes the trade-off numerical
(Theorem~\ref{thm:counts}).

Applications beyond the motivating story include conflict scheduling with a mandated minimum
number of slots (the sandwich gives an \emph{a priori} certified range, the abundance bound a
large solution space for randomised or preference-aware search); frequency assignment, where
$\Delta \le 5$ certifies six frequencies and $(q-d)^n$ lower-bounds the number of valid plans;
and complementarity diagnostics, where measuring $\Delta \le 5$ on a hundred-node network
certifies that the complement has a vertex of degree at least $16$.

\textbf{Limitations.} The model treats friendship as symmetric, unweighted and static, and
consistency as a hard constraint. The chromatic polynomial is the zero-temperature limit of the
antiferromagnetic Potts partition function, which suggests the natural soft generalisation
$Z_G(q,\beta) = \sum_c \prod_{x \sim y}\bigl(1 + (e^{-\beta}-1)\delta_{c(x),c(y)}\bigr)$, with
all results here recovered as $\beta \to \infty$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Brooks' theorem, emotional form]
If a finite connected social network is neither a clique nor an odd friendship circle, then
$\chi(G) \le \Delta(G)$, hence $\emoc(G) \le \max\{\Delta(G),3\}$.
\end{conjecture}
The greedy bound wastes exactly one color, and the waste can be recovered by ordering the
population so that the last person colored has two already-colored neighbours that are themselves
non-adjacent --- a local surgery the induction of Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} can carry, since it
never refers to the structure on the remaining people.

\begin{conjecture}[Chromatic polynomial of the friendship circle]
For $n \ge 3$ and every $q$, $P_{C_n}(q) = (q-1)^n + (-1)^n(q-1)$; consequently the six-emotion
count of an $n$-person friendship circle is $5^n + (-1)^n\cdot 5$.
\end{conjecture}
The closed form is a transfer-matrix trace, $P_{C_n}(q) = \operatorname{tr}((J-I)^n)$ for the
$q \times q$ all-ones-minus-identity matrix, so deletion--contraction reduces the cyclic count to
the path count by a gluing bijection identifying the endpoints.

\begin{conjecture}[Degeneracy abundance]
If a social network is $k$-degenerate --- every sub-community contains somebody with at most $k$
friends inside it --- then $P_G(q) \ge (q-k)^{n}$ and $\emoc(G) \le \max\{k+1,3\}$.
\end{conjecture}
Degeneracy is the right parameter for real social data, where a few hubs inflate $\Delta$ without
making the network hard to color; the induction of Theorem~\ref{thm:abundance} should go through
verbatim in a degeneracy order, since each vertex is constrained only by its at most $k$
back-neighbours.

\section{Conclusion}

The chromatic counting function of a social network, read under a three-category floor, obeys a
tidy set of laws: antitone in friendships, monotone in the palette, with an up-set support above
the floor that makes the emotional chromatic number a genuine threshold; that threshold is
sandwiched between the clique number and the maximum degree plus one, with both inequalities
strict for the six-person hub-and-circle network; and where the sandwich guarantees existence, a
counting refinement guarantees exponential abundance. Closed forms for cliques-with-bystanders
make a hundred-network census exact, and a complementarity law $n \le \emoc(G)\emoc(\cbar)$ shows
that emotional simplicity cannot be had on both sides of a relation at once. The chromatic
polynomial simultaneously measures how much diversity a system of constraints forces and how much
freedom it leaves --- and in a social network those two quantities are inversely coupled by the
size of its tightest clique.

